{"calculation": "", "answer": "The global economic crisis around 2008 was sparked by a decline in U.S. housing prices, which had doubled since 2000. This decline led to many mortgage loans being of poor quality, as borrowers took larger loans they could not afford to repay. With housing prices falling, the value of these mortgages often exceeded the price of the houses, giving borrowers an incentive to default. These poor-quality mortgages were bundled into complex securities, making it difficult to assess their value. When the quality of these assets on other banks' balance sheets became uncertain, banks became reluctant to lend to each other, leading to a financial crisis. This financial crisis then turned into an economic crisis, with stock prices collapsing and output growth turning negative in the U.S. and other countries, as trade and financial channels spread the effects globally. The crisis was not contained to the U.S., as European and emerging market stock prices also decreased significantly, and the U.S. recession quickly became a world recession, with advanced economies experiencing negative growth and emerging economies growing at a much slower rate than before the crisis.",
